In the discussion, James sought a summary of the paper "A multi-model assessment of inequality and climate change" by Emmerling et al. (2024) for a report targeting Ministries of Finance. The paper explores the interrelation between climate change and inequality using eight integrated assessment models (IAMs) and highlights that climate impacts could increase the Gini index by 1.4 points by 2100. It emphasizes the role of well-designed carbon revenue redistribution schemes, like equal per capita transfers, which can offset short-term increases in inequality. A paragraph was crafted to communicate the macroeconomic risks associated with 'No Climate Policy' and the benefits of adaptation under the 'Paris Agreement with EPC.' This summary encapsulated the key findings from multiple models to reinforce policy recommendations for fiscal planning and equitable climate action.
